@@s001 Strict, by-name, lazy
@@s002 ora:computed now,ok:result,spento:never computed
@@s003 Same call with two kinds of parameter. <code>id</code> prints its argument and returns it, so one sees when it is computed.
@@s004 <b>Strict</b> (if1): before entering the function Scala computes the arguments. id(23) → prints 23.
@@s005 And id(42) → prints 42, even if it will not be needed.
@@s006 Only now does it enter: if true then 23 else 42 = 23. Output "23 42" (checked).
@@s007 <b>By-name</b> (if3): it enters at once, the arguments are recipes. cond is true: only the chosen branch is computed, id(23) → prints 23.
@@s008 id(42) is <b>never</b> computed. Output "23". A <code>lazy val</code> would remember the value after the first use, instead of recomputing it.
@@s009 ora:condition,due:recipe run,no:recipe never run,ok:value of res
@@s010 ora:print,ok:value remembered
@@s011 ora:computation of x,ok:result
@@s012 ok:computed,spento:never computed
@@s013 no:wrong,ok:correct
@@s014 Strict, by-name, lazy
@@s015 When is the argument of a function computed? <b>Before</b> entering (strict), <b>every time</b> you use it (by-name), or <b>the first time</b> you use it and then no more (lazy). Scala has all three, and the LazyLists of this week rest on the last two.
@@s016 Three versions of <code>if</code> (Strictness.scala)
@@s017 <code>id</code> prints its argument and returns it: so we see <b>when</b> an argument is computed.
@@s018 if1: ordinary parameters (strict)
@@s019 Ordinary parameters: Scala computes the arguments <b>before</b> entering the function.
@@s020 It computes the first argument.
@@s021 And also the second, even if it will not be needed.
@@s022 Only now does it enter. It has printed <b>both</b> (checked): <code>if1</code> is <b>strict</b>.
@@s023 if2: functions without arguments (thunks)
@@s024 Each branch is a function that takes nothing: a <b>recipe</b> (thunk). Inside, <code>onTrue()</code> runs it.
@@s025 I pass two recipes. Creating a function does not run its body: nothing printed.
@@s026 Only the recipe of the chosen branch is run.
@@s027 42 has never been computed (checked). Running a thunk is called <b>forcing</b> it.
@@s028 if3: by-name parameters
@@s029 <code>=> A</code> (arrow with nothing on the left): a <b>by-name</b> parameter. Scala creates the recipe for you and runs it each time you write the name. No <code>()</code>.
@@s030 The call looks like that of <code>if1</code>…
@@s031 …but it behaves like <code>if2</code>: it prints only 23 (checked). Same meaning, more convenient syntax.
