@@s044 The trap of the poll
@@s045 <b>What is <code>res</code> worth?</b> Answer: <b>() — the Unit value</b>. 42 &lt; 22 is false, so the recipe of the false branch is run, which <b>prints</b> "b". But <code>println</code> returns <code>Unit</code>, so <code>res = ()</code> (checked: <i>res = ()</i>). Printing is an effect, not the value.
@@s046 Prints b, but res = ()
@@s047 <code>lazy val</code>: computes once, then remembers
@@s048 A by-name parameter is recomputed <b>every time</b> you use it. If you want to compute it only once, put it in a <code>lazy val</code>.
@@s049 When it is computed
@@s050 Defined. It prints nothing: nobody has used it yet.
@@s051 The program goes on. <code>pesante</code> is not yet computed.
@@s052 First use: computes.
@@s053 Second use: remembers the result, does not recompute (checked).
@@s054 convoluted(print(&quot;A&quot;), print(&quot;B&quot;)) — slide 6
@@s055 Two by-name parameters, put into two <code>lazy val</code>. Then I use each twice.
@@s056 First use of <code>cacheA</code>: forces <code>a</code>. The order of the definitions does not matter, the order of use does.
@@s057 First use of <code>cacheB</code>.
@@s058 Already in cache.
@@s059 Prints "AB" (checked). Without <code>lazy val</code> it would print "ABAB".
@@s060 Written
@@s061 When it is computed
