@@s066 at first use
@@s067 <code>def x = …</code>
@@s068 at each use
@@s069 every time
@@s070 First use computes, then remembers
@@s071 The three strategies, defined
@@s072 Call-by-value (strict)
@@s073 The arguments are computed <b>before</b> the call; in the body their value is substituted.
@@s074 Call-by-name
@@s075 The arguments are <b>not</b> computed: in the body the expression itself is substituted, and it is computed at each use.
@@s076 Lazy
@@s077 Lazy = call-by-name + <b>memory</b>: the expression is computed at first use and the result is reused afterwards.
@@s078 Strict function (slide 4)
@@s079 <span class="m">f(x)</span> is strict if, for every expression <span class="m">x</span> that does not terminate or fails, <span class="m">f(x)</span> also does not terminate or fails. <code>if</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> are not strict: <code>false &amp;&amp; crash()</code> is <code>false</code>.
@@s080 In a <b>pure</b> program the three strategies give the same result: only time and memory change. With effects (prints, random numbers) the results become different, which is why laziness is used with pure code.
@@s081 when: extending the language with a by-name parameter
@@s082 The block between braces is the second argument.
@@s083 
@@s084 Strict: the block is run <b>before</b> entering (checked).
@@s085 By-name: the block runs only if the <code>if</code> reaches it. This is how "new constructs" such as <code>withScope</code> of Apache Spark are written (slides 9–12).
@@s086 By value, by name, lazy
@@s087 What it is for
@@s088 <b>Non-strict functions</b>: the default of <code>getOrElse(default: => B)</code> is computed only with <code>None</code>.
@@s089 <b>Large data</b>: computing only the parts that are really needed.
@@s090 <b>Generators</b>: the infinite list of naturals, of prime numbers, of random numbers (the <a href="s04-lazylist.html">LazyLists</a>).
@@s091 <b>Separating what from how</b>: you describe a large calculation and run only the piece you need.
@@s092 Four uses of non-strict evaluation
